    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this work the pressure build-up behavior of a two-well, solution gas-drive oil reservoir is studied by simulating the reservoir system on a digital computer, The purpose of the study is to determine: (l) how the pressure build-up in a shut-in well might be analyzed to estimate reservoir pressure and permeability when the conditions of two-phase flow and well interference exist; and (2) whether the pressure behavior of a producing well which is located near a well undergoing pressure build-up can be used to develop information about average reservoir pressure and permeability.The mathematical simulation is based on two simultaneous nonlinear partial differential equations which describe the flow of the fluids in the reservoir. There is one equation for the oil phase, and one equation for the gas phase. Each of these equations is a combination of Darcy's Law and the continuity equation. As boundary conditions either the pressure or the production rate is specified as a function of time at each well, and the outer reservoir boundary is required to be impermeable. Subject to these boundary conditions, the flow equations are solved for the pressure and saturation distributions as functions of time. These equations are rearranged algebraically so that one can be considered as the pressure equation and the other as the saturation equation. After rearrangement a transformation of coordinates is made to facilitate the study of the pressure behavior in the vicinity of the wells. These differential equations are then represented by finite difference equations, the pressure equation being in implicit form, the saturation equation in explicit form. The finite difference equations are linearized and solved by well-known techniques of numerical analysis.The simulation has been used with a given set of fluid properties to study eight different pressure build-up tests involving five production histories. The results for the two-well system indicate that the pressure response of the shut-in well may be used to estimate the average formation permeability and pressure. The oil and gas permeabilities would be estimated by extending the analysis used for single-phase fluids. To estimate the average pressure of the drainage region, the drainage region would be treated as though it had a simple geometric shape. Then the method proposed by Matthews, Brons, and Hazebroek would be used. The results of this study also indicate that the response of the producing well in the two-well system cannot be used to estimate formation pressure or permeability, even though such a calculation theoretically could be made for single-phase, infinite systems.",
